        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 10878,
            "sid": 3,
            "position_x": 1070,
            "position_y": 29,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 1096,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Takes a DIFFERENT position from each row (or column), using a tensor of positions shaped like the input.\n\nThe other selectors take the same positions from every row. This one reads its indices row by row: row 0 of the result is taken from row 0 of the input at the positions in row 0 of the indices, and so on. It is the partner of the nodes that MAKE such positions - t.argsort, t.argmax:\n\n  t.argsort 1, then t.take_along_dim 1 with the same tensor, sorts each\n  row (that is the demo).\n  Feed the indices from one tensor and the values from ANOTHER, and you\n  reorder the second by the first - \"sort the angles by the speeds\", or\n  \"the angle of each joint at the moment it was fastest\".\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.take_along_dim <dim: int>\nDefault dim 0. The dim box is always shown.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.take_along_dim 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values (a list or numpy array is converted to a float32 tensor). Sends on arrival. This is the only inlet that sends.\n\nindices in:\nThe positions, as whole numbers, with the SAME NUMBER OF DIMENSIONS as the input. Along dim they may be any length (that is how many values each row gives); along every other dimension they must match the input or be 1 (a single row of indices then applies to every row). t.argmax 1 removes the dimension it worked along - put it back with t.unsqueeze 1 before using it here. Wrong shapes print an error in the console and nothing is sent. This inlet is cold: send the indices FIRST. Nothing is sent until indices have arrived.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich dimension the positions run along: 1 positions within each row, 0 positions within each column. Negative counts from the end (-1 is the last dimension, so on a matrix -1 is 1 and -2 is 0). A dimension the tensor does not have sends nothing.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe values taken, shaped like the indices.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nClick t.rand for a fresh 6 x 8 tensor; var holds it and the metro re-sends it ten times a second. t.argsort 1 gives, for each row, the column of its smallest value, then the next smallest, and so on - the middle heat_map, numbered 0 to 7, different in every row. t.take_along_dim 1 reads each row at its own positions, so every row of the result runs dark to bright. Click sort_columns and both nodes switch to dim 0: t.argsort then gives each column's positions, 0 to 5 from its smallest value, and every column of the result runs dark to bright from top to bottom. sort_rows switches them back to dim 1. Tick descending on t.argsort and the rows run bright to dark instead.\n\nRELATED:\nt.take uses flat positions. t.index_select takes the same positions from every row. t.argsort and t.argmax make the positions. np.argsort is the numpy argsort; numpy's np.[] cannot take a different position per row.",
                    "value_type": "string"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1080,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "string"
                }
            },
            "text": "Takes a DIFFERENT position from each row (or column), using a tensor of positions shaped like the input.\n\nThe other selectors take the same positions from every row. This one reads its indices row by row: row 0 of the result is taken from row 0 of the input at the positions in row 0 of the indices, and so on. It is the partner of the nodes that MAKE such positions - t.argsort, t.argmax:\n\n  t.argsort 1, then t.take_along_dim 1 with the same tensor, sorts each\n  row (that is the demo).\n  Feed the indices from one tensor and the values from ANOTHER, and you\n  reorder the second by the first - \"sort the angles by the speeds\", or\n  \"the angle of each joint at the moment it was fastest\".\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.take_along_dim <dim: int>\nDefault dim 0. The dim box is always shown.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.take_along_dim 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values (a list or numpy array is converted to a float32 tensor). Sends on arrival. This is the only inlet that sends.\n\nindices in:\nThe positions, as whole numbers, with the SAME NUMBER OF DIMENSIONS as the input. Along dim they may be any length (that is how many values each row gives); along every other dimension they must match the input or be 1 (a single row of indices then applies to every row). t.argmax 1 removes the dimension it worked along - put it back with t.unsqueeze 1 before using it here. Wrong shapes print an error in the console and nothing is sent. This inlet is cold: send the indices FIRST. Nothing is sent until indices have arrived.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich dimension the positions run along: 1 positions within each row, 0 positions within each column. Negative counts from the end (-1 is the last dimension, so on a matrix -1 is 1 and -2 is 0). A dimension the tensor does not have sends nothing.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe values taken, shaped like the indices.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nClick t.rand for a fresh 6 x 8 tensor; var holds it and the metro re-sends it ten times a second. t.argsort 1 gives, for each row, the column of its smallest value, then the next smallest, and so on - the middle heat_map, numbered 0 to 7, different in every row. t.take_along_dim 1 reads each row at its own positions, so every row of the result runs dark to bright. Click sort_columns and both nodes switch to dim 0: t.argsort then gives each column's positions, 0 to 5 from its smallest value, and every column of the result runs dark to bright from top to bottom. sort_rows switches them back to dim 1. Tick descending on t.argsort and the rows run bright to dark instead.\n\nRELATED:\nt.take uses flat positions. t.index_select takes the same positions from every row. t.argsort and t.argmax make the positions. np.argsort is the numpy argsort; numpy's np.[] cannot take a different position per row."
        },
